    "name_suffix": "_iste-2026",
    "offer_browser_tts": "false",
    "teacher_markdown": "**Intent:** Students start to see a peek \"under the hood\" without being overwhelmed by the raw HTML and CSS code. The Tag Inspector makes this discovery-based rather than lecture-based. The key insight to reinforce is that every visual element on a page is an HTML element with some CSS rules applied to it, and knowing those names is how you start directing AI precisely.\r\n\r\n**Circulation questions:**\r\n\r\n- \"What HTML tag did you find? What do you think it does?\"  \r\n- \"What CSS rule did you find? What do you think it controls?\"  \r\n- \"If you wanted to change the color of that element, what would you tell AI Tutor?\"\r\n\r\n**Expected mistakes and misconceptions:**\r\n\r\n- Students may hover quickly without reading what the Tag Inspector shows \\- encourage them to pause on one element and try to understand it before moving on  \r\n- Students may ask AI Tutor to make changes at this stage rather than just asking questions \\- redirect them: \"this level is about understanding what's there, not changing it yet\"  \r\n- Students may not be sure what question to ask AI Tutor \\- suggest they pick one tag or property and ask \"what does this do?\"",
